hi there steve kaufman here it's a  beautiful day the sun is shining right  at me here  uh in my little room and i have  something i want to really i want to  talk about i'm going to talk about it in  english and maybe in other languages as  well  i'll tell you right up front this uh  video is in english someone complained  that my previous video in french  uh it wasn't clearly indicated that was  in french and he had to listen through  quite a bit of it and fast forward to  figure out that it was all in french so  i will try to be a little more careful  in that regard  i posted on my blog the linguist on  language about a  concept called auding a-u-d-i-g-i-n-g  and oracy o-r-a-c-y  this isn't like odding as opposed to  reading so listening instead of reading  oracy instead of literacy oracy being  the ability to hear well and speak well  as opposed to literacy which is the  ability to read well and write well  and there's all kinds of evidence that  the ability to to hear and to understand  to when we're listening is extremely  important  and of course the ability to speak well  is important that these are along with  the literacy skills very important to  the success of a society and the success  of  individuals in that society and i have  commented often here that the key to to  successful literacy  is is  the opportunity to hear a lot of words  and we know that children from families  where the parents  speak to them on a variety of subjects  that those children do better at school  they read better  maybe not immediately because in the  very early ages everyone is being taught  their letters and  and so they're all kind of at the same  level but as soon as that  initial part of literacy learning is  behind them those kids who have heard a  lot of words  just learn just read a lot better  because obviously it's a lot easier to  read words that you already know  because you've heard them  and similarly kids who are familiar with  a lot of subjects that have a lot of  general knowledge from listening to  their parents or others  they'll have an easier time reading  because obviously if you're familiar  with  the subject matter that you're reading  about you're going to have an easier  time reading and there's all kinds of  tests that have been uh  done  that show that  uh if you have two groups of  readers or let's say  learners learning a foreign language  and you give one group  you know a word list a vocabulary list  to study beforehand and the other one  doesn't get that list and then they both  have to read the same text there's no  appreciable difference in other words  you can't suddenly start memorizing  stuff on the other hand what really  matters is how familiar people are with  the text that they're about to read so  whether it be in foreign language  learning or literacy  the more words you have and the more  familiar you are with different subjects  the better off you're going to do  and so where i'm coming from on all of  this is you know obviously reading is a  major subject at school people are  taught to read  i wonder whether we aren't missing the  boat in that we don't deliberately teach  people to listen and work on their  listening or auditing skills  and and what's really happened that can  make this much easier now than ever  before  of course is the appearance of these  little mp3 players  and i know that my language learning has  taken off since i was able to get a hold  of an mp3 player because i can listen  while doing other things  what's more i notice  that i have a  better time concentrating  when i'm active  whereas if i sit in a chair and try to  listen i tend to lose focus  but if i'm doing the dishes  even if i'm driving the car  if i'm running around a field or jogging  or whatever i'm doing it surprisingly i  focus better  on the content of what i'm listening to  so what i want to propose  because in schools  most kids don't like sitting in a  classroom and being  you know taught at  um we also know that there are problems  in all  modern societies with obesity and  inactive children  i think there should be us a program  every day in school  where children are given something  useful to listen to  uh relating to what they are studying  and that they are then asked to run  around the field for half an hour  listening to this now a lot of people  say well the kids won't listen and  they'll listen to music well first of  all you can control you can give them  an an mp3 player that only has  whatever it is you want them to listen  to on that mp3 player so there's no way  they're going to listen to music  second of all you say they won't do it  but they read and if you think about it  if we didn't have books and reading and  writing  and people had only ever listened and  then you say okay now we want you to sit  down and stick your nose at this piece  of paper and just read it  there would be a lot of resistance  so listening to something is not  necessarily a more difficult concept  than reading  and particularly if you add in the  opportunity to go and run  and i think we should train people to  get better at listening and increase  their vocabulary and give them a variety  of interesting things to listen to and i  think that  from my experience with link and with my  own language learning  that people will learn more if they  listen and read  than if they're in a classroom with the  teacher trying to explain a bunch of  stuff  let the kids hear about it on their own  let them hear it more than once let them  hear it  in different formats stories articles  dialogues  going over some of the same material but  let them just absorb it in a passive way  while active  i'm convinced that people would learn  more  if they if they learned in that way if  that opportunity were made available to  them plus they would develop a skill a  habit which i think can be very very  useful in later life and that's the the  habit of learning while doing other  things so we'd we'd achieve several  things here we'd we'd  make auditing or listening a specific  skill that we teach in school  we would do it in combination with  physical exercise so we're also getting  the kids more active and if it's winter  they run around the gym they don't have  find some run on a treadmill it doesn't  matter  reduce the number of class hours where  the kids are being unruly and the  teacher is mostly concerned with trying  to maintain discipline in the classroom  and so we do all of these things the  kids will learn more in my opinion now  granted i haven't tested any of these  theories but it's just something that's  really come at me in the last few days  and we develop a skill that can be very  useful  throughout one's life  and if in all of that  and  i don't hesitate to say if we combine  the listing with something like link  where we people can deliberately  increase their vocabulary then what we  would do what we would end up doing is  increasing the vocabulary  of  these kids and eventually adults we  would increase their  familiarity with a variety of subjects  we would improve their ability to listen  we would improve their auditing skills  we would improve their oracy skills we  would also improve their literacy skills  we would have more successful people  more productive economy i mean the  overwhelming majority of of people who  are in prison  are people with very poor  literacy skills and probably as well  poor oracy or auditing or listening  skills so there you have it uh i'm very  interested in get in getting some  reaction to this  and uh i'll probably uh do it up in a  few other languages to see if we can get  some interest from uh speakers of those  languages as well so let me know what  you think maybe i'm completely  you know out of my mind which i probably  am half the time anyway  thanks for listening bye for now
 

